WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.179 --> 00:00:01.920
Hey thoughty2 here.

00:00:01.920 --> 00:00:08.270
Over the past 3500 years, mankind has been
at war with itself, to various degrees, pretty

00:00:08.270 --> 00:00:10.310
much constantly.

00:00:10.310 --> 00:00:18.460
In fact, we’ve only managed 268 years of
world peace in all that time because there’s

00:00:18.460 --> 00:00:23.490
always some other group of people with land
you want, a god you dislike or just because

00:00:23.490 --> 00:00:24.490
it’s Monday.

00:00:24.490 --> 00:00:26.410
And we’re getting really good at war.

00:00:26.410 --> 00:00:32.509
The 20th century was a record-breaker with
over 100 million dead people proving we had

00:00:32.509 --> 00:00:38.620
really turned into pros; none of this swords
and horseback nonsense anymore.

00:00:38.620 --> 00:00:41.760
But we’re also more civilized aren’t we?

00:00:41.760 --> 00:00:46.770
Only a barbarian would cut a prisoners tongue
out and put their head on a stick; a drone

00:00:46.770 --> 00:00:52.480
and some laser guided missiles are a far more
cultured way of doing things.

00:00:52.480 --> 00:00:57.570
And since we fancied ourselves as civilized,
we decided to draw up a rule book about what

00:00:57.570 --> 00:01:02.530
you can and cannot do when it comes to killing
millions of people; I know, but there’s

00:01:02.530 --> 00:01:05.710
a right way and wrong way to do it.

00:01:05.710 --> 00:01:09.780
So lock up your guillotines and hide away
your mustard gas, because you’re not allowed

00:01:09.780 --> 00:01:11.549
to use them anymore, you naughty tyrant.

00:01:11.549 --> 00:01:16.869
As we discover, what is a war crime?

00:01:16.869 --> 00:01:24.100
For thousands of years, there have been rules,
both written and unwritten, about how warfare

00:01:24.100 --> 00:01:26.030
should be practiced.

00:01:26.030 --> 00:01:31.820
The Mahabharata, an epic poem from ancient
India, contains a passage saying “War should

00:01:31.820 --> 00:01:36.900
be waged for the sake of conquest; one should
not be enraged toward an enemy who is not

00:01:36.900 --> 00:01:38.740
trying to kill him.”

00:01:38.740 --> 00:01:43.420
This is the ancient Indian way of saying;
don’t hate the player, hate the game.

00:01:43.420 --> 00:01:48.660
The bible also contains passages on war, such
as banning people from cutting down trees

00:01:48.660 --> 00:01:51.729
outside the city they’re laying siege to.

00:01:51.729 --> 00:01:57.890
Apparently “thou shalt not kill” was open
to so much interpretation that the sub-clauses

00:01:57.890 --> 00:02:00.580
even cover gardening before a massacre.

00:02:00.580 --> 00:02:05.799
But the earliest record we have of punishment
for a war crime, which wasn’t just the victors

00:02:05.799 --> 00:02:12.529
punishing the losers, was when Peter von Hagenbach
was beheaded by the Holy Roman Empire for

00:02:12.529 --> 00:02:18.169
his treatment of civilians during an uprising
in Breisach in 1474.

00:02:18.169 --> 00:02:23.040
The reason, they said, was that his crimes
were those “he as a knight was deemed to

00:02:23.040 --> 00:02:24.849
have a duty to prevent.”

00:02:24.849 --> 00:02:30.810
It’s also the earliest example of a case
involving ‘command responsibility’, since

00:02:30.810 --> 00:02:34.909
he claimed, unsuccessfully, that he was just
following orders.

00:02:34.909 --> 00:02:41.199
The first international war crime agreement
signed was the Strasbourg Agreement of 27

00:02:41.199 --> 00:02:43.489
August 1675.

00:02:43.489 --> 00:02:48.310
It was between France and the Holy Roman Empire,
which at this point was basically Germany,

00:02:48.310 --> 00:02:51.510
to forbid the use of poisoned bullets.

00:02:51.510 --> 00:02:56.469
You’d think that this was the earliest form
of chemical warfare but in fact it had been

00:02:56.469 --> 00:02:59.269
happening for over 2000 years.

00:02:59.269 --> 00:03:05.609
In 600 BC, the Athenians used a heart toxin
taken from the Hellebore plant to poison the

00:03:05.609 --> 00:03:08.989
city of Kirrha during a siege.

00:03:08.989 --> 00:03:15.040
Around 250 AD, the Persians battled the Romans
in Syria by burning sulfur and bitumen to

00:03:15.040 --> 00:03:16.219
suffocate them.

00:03:16.219 --> 00:03:22.579
And in 1250, when the Chinese were fighting
Genghis Khan, they made bombs out of gunpowder,

00:03:22.579 --> 00:03:29.589
feces and a variety of poisons like arsenic
and aconite root which caused nausea and convulsions.

00:03:29.589 --> 00:03:33.889
What a lovely sounding cocktail, but I’ll
take a martini thanks.

00:03:33.889 --> 00:03:38.299
At the end of the 19th century, many of the
major powers were beginning to think that

00:03:38.299 --> 00:03:43.239
maybe there was a better way to do things
and they decided to adopt an international

00:03:43.239 --> 00:03:48.069
version of the Leiber Code, which had been
signed by Abraham Lincoln after the American

00:03:48.069 --> 00:03:49.169
Civil War.

00:03:49.169 --> 00:03:55.510
It outlawed the use of torture to get confessions,
killing prisoners, using poisons and putting

00:03:55.510 --> 00:03:59.409
proximity mines on respawn locations if you
were playing Goldeneye.

00:03:59.409 --> 00:04:06.229
Russian Tsar Nicholas II was instrumental
in setting up the Hague Convention of 1899

00:04:06.229 --> 00:04:10.939
where a lot of this was agreed on and signed
by 26 countries.

00:04:10.939 --> 00:04:17.359
It also forbade bullets that expanded on impact,
using balloons for bombing and they agreed

00:04:17.359 --> 00:04:20.069
not to attack marked hospital ships.

00:04:20.069 --> 00:04:25.440
There was a follow up in 1907 that covered
a lot more maritime law.

00:04:25.440 --> 00:04:31.010
Once we had all those laws neatly written
on paper, we decided to celebrate by breaking

00:04:31.010 --> 00:04:37.680
them all in the horrific slaughter of World
War I, where mustard gas filled the trenches,

00:04:37.680 --> 00:04:42.710
prisoners were used as forced labour and nobody
gave a flying monkey about any of the laws

00:04:42.710 --> 00:04:44.370
of combat.

00:04:44.370 --> 00:04:50.180
Henry Dunant was a Swiss businessman who became
the first recipient of the Nobel Peace Prize

00:04:50.180 --> 00:04:57.729
in 1901 for his humanitarian work which led
to the 1864 Geneva Convention and the setting

00:04:57.729 --> 00:04:58.960
up of the Red Cross.

00:04:58.960 --> 00:05:04.289
This first Geneva convention agreed to protect
all the people not actively trying to kill

00:05:04.289 --> 00:05:10.110
each other such as the wounded, the civilians,
the shipwrecked, the POWs.

00:05:10.110 --> 00:05:15.300
We needed multiple other Geneva Conventions
over the next hundred years since each new

00:05:15.300 --> 00:05:20.490
war proved that we were absolutely terrible
at actually following them.

00:05:20.490 --> 00:05:25.270
But they are the cornerstone of most current
humanitarian law.

00:05:25.270 --> 00:05:29.780
If you break a law in your own country, then
it’s generally clear what the punishment

00:05:29.780 --> 00:05:37.220
is, but none of these conventions have a clear
punishment written into them so how well do

00:05:37.220 --> 00:05:38.560
they really work?

00:05:38.560 --> 00:05:42.210
I think you probably already know the answer;
they don’t.

00:05:42.210 --> 00:05:47.840
It’s not to say they have no value, we have
to have some common agreement on what is acceptable

00:05:47.840 --> 00:05:53.330
or not but they are extremely difficult to
enforce.

00:05:53.330 --> 00:05:59.039
If you look at WWII, then the Nuremberg trials
famously held various Nazi officers accountable

00:05:59.039 --> 00:06:00.900
for their actions.

00:06:00.900 --> 00:06:07.360
But what of the 10,000 prisoners who died
of dehydration and malnutrition in the Rheinwiesenlager

00:06:07.360 --> 00:06:10.880
prison camps, run by the Americans in occupied
Germany?

00:06:10.880 --> 00:06:16.990
Or how about the 25,000 civilians who died
when the British firebombed Dresden?

00:06:16.990 --> 00:06:24.139
Or the hundreds of thousands, possibly millions,
of Germans raped by Soviet and other Allied

00:06:24.139 --> 00:06:26.919
forces after Berlin fell.

00:06:26.919 --> 00:06:32.800
Yes, the allies weren’t all goody two-shoes,
this was war after all.

00:06:32.800 --> 00:06:36.810
Even now, the Allies are pretty stubborn about
dealing with what they were up to.

00:06:36.810 --> 00:06:40.400
And tend to ignore the atrocities they committed
during war.

00:06:40.400 --> 00:06:46.800
It’s often been said that the victors write
the history books but they also dictate their

00:06:46.800 --> 00:06:50.340
own punishment, or lack of it in most cases,
when it comes to war crimes.

00:06:50.340 --> 00:06:55.699
The Japanese are one of the few countries
to admit to breaches of the Geneva Convention

00:06:55.699 --> 00:07:00.430
particularly to the surrounding Asian nations
such as South Korea and China.

00:07:00.430 --> 00:07:05.889
But an apology is mostly all that happened
so I’m not sure a fear of saying sorry is

00:07:05.889 --> 00:07:10.629
going to keep Kim Jong Un’s chubby finger
off the nuclear button.

00:07:10.629 --> 00:07:14.070
So has any progress been made?

00:07:14.070 --> 00:07:17.879
Are we any closer to really enforcing these
rules of engagement?

00:07:17.879 --> 00:07:20.419
Well, maybe a little.

00:07:20.419 --> 00:07:25.610
When it comes to using illegal weapons, we’ve
realized that the best way to stop people

00:07:25.610 --> 00:07:30.339
is to not let them have them in the first
place.

00:07:30.339 --> 00:07:35.990
Just like if you own a gun you’re probably
more likely to use it than someone who doesn’t

00:07:35.990 --> 00:07:36.990
own a gun.

00:07:36.990 --> 00:07:37.990
But I won’t go into that.

00:07:37.990 --> 00:07:42.910
For nuclear weapons, the 5 members of the
UN Security Council possess them “legally”,

00:07:42.910 --> 00:07:47.360
that’s China, Russia, France, UK and the
US.

00:07:47.360 --> 00:07:53.879
And only four others have them, that we know
of; India, Pakistan, Israel and North Korea,

00:07:53.879 --> 00:07:59.340
but they are not part of the treaty so there’s
not a lot we can really do about it except

00:07:59.340 --> 00:08:02.900
ask them really really nicely not to end the
world.

00:08:02.900 --> 00:08:08.719
But according to the treaties for chemical
and biological weapons, all stockpiles should

00:08:08.719 --> 00:08:10.039
have been destroyed.

00:08:10.039 --> 00:08:16.880
No one should keep some spare ones around
for a rainy day and any country in the treaty

00:08:16.880 --> 00:08:23.180
is supposed to be able to ask the UN to send
inspectors to any other country to check they’re

00:08:23.180 --> 00:08:26.340
not hiding Sarin gas behind the biscuit tin.

00:08:26.340 --> 00:08:31.620
For many of the wars in recent memory, including
the current one in Syria, the use or suspected

00:08:31.620 --> 00:08:37.360
possession of chemical and biological weaponry
has been the key reason given for a US and

00:08:37.360 --> 00:08:38.370
British intervention.

00:08:38.370 --> 00:08:44.219
And, as you all know, all of those wars have
been a resounding success and everyone lived

00:08:44.219 --> 00:08:46.560
happily ever after.

00:08:46.560 --> 00:08:53.370
So yeah, international war conventions really
do work, it’s all totally fine and I’m

00:08:53.370 --> 00:08:57.290
just building that bunker in my garden for
fun, no other reason.

00:08:57.290 --> 00:09:12.850
Thanks for watching, subscribe 
for more Thoughty2.

